### What is a Bounded Context?

A Bounded Context is a DDD concept that defines an explicit boundary within which a particular domain model is consistent and unambiguous. Inside the boundary, every term has exactly one meaning.

```java
package com.bank.deposits.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// In the Deposits bounded context: "Account" = deposit account
public class Account {
    private String accountId;   // e.g., SAV2001
    private String holderName;  // Rajesh
    private BigDecimal balance; // 500000.00
    private String type;        // SAV, CUR
}
```

```java
package com.bank.lending.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// In the Lending bounded context: "Account" = loan account
public class Account {
    private String loanAccountId; // LOAN-001
    private BigDecimal principal;  // 2000000.00
    private BigDecimal interestRate; // 8.5%
    private int tenureMonths;     // 240
}
```

```text
Same word "Account", different meanings:
  Deposits: balance, holder, type
  Lending: principal, interest rate, tenure
  Cards: limit, outstanding, due date

These are DIFFERENT classes in DIFFERENT services. This is deliberate.
```

### What is ubiquitous language?

The ubiquitous language is the exact words the team uses in code, docs, and conversation. It is consistent only inside the boundary.

```text
Deposits team language:
  "account" = savings or current account
  "balance" = current available amount
  "holder" = person who owns the account

Lending team language:
  "account" = loan account
  "principal" = original loan amount
  "EMI" = equated monthly installment
  "sanction" = approve the loan

These are different languages. Each is correct INSIDE its context.
```

### How does bounded context map to microservices?

Each bounded context becomes one service with a private database. This is why Bounded Context and Database-per-Service reinforce each other.

```text
  Bounded Context          Service              Database
  ─────────────────       ───────────────       ──────────────
  Deposits Context   ->   Account Service  ->   account_db
  Lending Context    ->   Loan Service     ->   loan_db
  Payment Context    ->   Payment Service  ->   payment_db
  Notification Ctx   ->   Notif Service    ->   notification_db

The boundary in the domain model = the boundary in deployment = the boundary in data.
```

### What are context maps?

Context maps describe how bounded contexts relate to each other.

```java
package com.bank.lending.adapter;

import com.bank.lending.model.FundsAvailability;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

// Anti-Corruption Layer: translates Deposits context into Lending terms.
// The Deposits model never leaks into the Lending model.
@Component
public class DepositsAntiCorruptionLayer {

    private final DepositsClient depositsClient;

    public DepositsAntiCorruptionLayer(DepositsClient depositsClient) {
        this.depositsClient = depositsClient;
    }

    public FundsAvailability checkFundsForEmiDeduction(String accountId) {
        // Call Deposits Service -> get DepositAccount (their model)
        var depositAccount = depositsClient.getAccount(accountId);
        // Translate into Lending's own model
        return new FundsAvailability(
            accountId,
            depositAccount.getBalance(),
            depositAccount.getBalance().compareTo(
                java.math.BigDecimal.ZERO) > 0);
    }
}
```

```text
Context map types:
  Shared Kernel:     small model both teams agree to change together
  Customer-Supplier: one context depends on another, they negotiate
  Anti-Corruption Layer: translation so another context's model does not leak in
  Domain Events:     contexts react to events without sharing a model

At National Bank of India:
  Deposits publishes FundsDebited event.
  Lending consumes it through an anti-corruption layer
    that translates it into an EMI payment.
  Neither model contaminates the other.
```

### What are the advantages of bounded contexts?

| Advantage | Explanation |
|---|---|
| Clear boundaries | Each team knows exactly what they own |
| Autonomous teams | Teams work independently within their context |
| Consistent language | Reduces miscommunication: one word, one meaning |
| Independent evolution | Schema and code changes are internal to each context |
| Clean data ownership | Database-per-service follows naturally |

### What are the disadvantages of bounded contexts?

| Disadvantage | Explanation |
|---|---|
| Model duplication | Same real-world thing represented differently in each context |
| Translation overhead | Need anti-corruption layers and event mappers at borders |
| Wrong boundaries are costly | Redrawing boundaries once services are live is expensive |
| Coordination for cross-context operations | Sagas and eventual consistency instead of simple joins |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is drawing service boundaries without the domain model.

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

// WRONG: Split services without DDD. One shared "Account" model everywhere.
public class WrongBoundary {
    // SharedAccount class used by Deposits, Lending, Cards.
    // Every change touches every service.
    // Technically split, conceptually still a monolith.
}

// CORRECT: Each context has its own Account model.
public class CorrectBoundary {
    // DepositAccount in Deposits context.
    // LoanAccount in Lending context.
    // CreditCardAccount in Cards context.
    // Each evolves independently.
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A bounded context is first a modeling boundary and only then a deployment boundary. If you draw service boundaries without doing the domain modeling, you get technically separated services that still share concepts and therefore still change together, defeating the whole purpose.
